// M23 -- threads. `satellite.thread.new`, `start()` and `join()`.
//
// DESIGN §7.1 IS THE DONE-WHEN AND IT IS WRITTEN AS A RECEIPT FOR A FAILURE.
// The first satellite kept every local in one registry keyed by
// `<capsule>.<variable>`, and the section records what that cost:
//
//     "Concurrency is worse, and would not be seen coming. EIGHT THREADS
//      running a capsule with NO RECURSION AND NO SHARED STATE produced 1585
//      WRONG RESULTS OUT OF 1600."
//
// So the first thing this file does is run that exact shape and assert the
// other answer. Eight threads, 200 activations each, 1600 results, and 1600 of
// them right -- because DESIGN §7.2's frame per call has been the language's
// shape since M9 and this is the first program that could ever have told.
//
// WHAT EACH SECTION PROVES:
//
//   §1  the deferred call is DEFERRED. `new` packages and does not enter.
//   §2  `start()` does not wait and `join()` does.
//   §3  DESIGN §7.1's 1600, from eight threads at once.
//   §4  `satellite.library` is ONE variable seen by every thread.
//   §5  join() answers what the capsule returned.
//   §6  a thread is a reference type -- two names, one thread.
//
// THE REFUSALS ARE NOT HERE AND THAT IS DELIBERATE. S1401 through S1405 each
// stop the run, so a program cannot demonstrate more than one of them;
// tests/eval_test's `threads.cpp` raises all five, which is where a refusal
// belongs when the language has no way to catch one.

satellite.include(satellite)

// §1's witness. Writes its mark into a global so the caller can see WHEN it
// ran, which is the only way to tell a packaged call from a performed one.
satellite.capsule leave_a_mark(satellite.variable.string mark)
{
    satellite.library.marks = satellite.library.marks + mark
    satellite.return()
}

// §3's capsule, and it is DESIGN §7.1's shape exactly: NO RECURSION AND NO
// SHARED STATE. Every name in here is a local, so every activation needs its
// own storage, and a language that gave them one slot between them would
// return the last writer's answer to all of them.
//
// THE MULTIPLY IS WHAT MAKES A WRONG ANSWER VISIBLE. If `n` were shared, two
// threads interleaving would leave `doubled` holding the other thread's
// number, and 200 activations of 8 threads would agree on almost nothing.
satellite.capsule count_alone(satellite.variable.number from) satellite.returns(satellite.variable.number)
{
    satellite.variable.number right = 0
    satellite.variable.number i = 0
    satellite.statement.for (i = 0; i < 200; i = i + 1)
    {
        satellite.variable.number n = from * 1000 + i
        satellite.variable.number doubled = n + n
        satellite.statement.if (doubled == n * 2)
        {
            right = right + 1
        }
    }
    satellite.return(right)
}

// §4's capsule. Reads, adds and writes back a GLOBAL -- which is the one thing
// DESIGN §7.2 reserves for sharing: "permanent identity, CROSS-THREAD SHARING,
// lock-free reads and atomic read-modify-write are exactly what globals need".
//
// THE ASSERTION IS NOT ON THE TOTAL AND THE COMMENT SAYS WHY. `read, add,
// write` is three operations and satellite does not make them one, so two
// threads can interleave and lose an increment. That is a data race in the
// PROGRAM and not in the language, and it is exactly what a global is for --
// what this section asserts is that every thread saw the SAME variable, which
// is provable without a total: a global nobody shared would still hold 0.
satellite.capsule touch_the_global()
{
    satellite.library.touched = satellite.library.touched + 1
    satellite.return()
}

// §2's and §6's capsule. Sleeps a quarter second, then says it got there.
//
// THE GLOBAL IS THE CLOCK, AND IT IS THE CLOCK BECAUSE THE LANGUAGE HAS NO
// OTHER ONE. `satellite.time.now()` answers an instant and DESIGN §12 defers
// durations -- "time is an absolute instant only; subtraction yields a number
// of nanoseconds" -- so `now() - began` is S0711 and a satellite program cannot
// yet measure how long anything took. What it CAN see is whether something has
// happened yet, which is the claim §2 actually needs. MILESTONES/M23.md §3.4.
satellite.capsule slow_answer() satellite.returns(satellite.variable.number)
{
    satellite.time.sleep(0.25)
    satellite.library.arrived = satellite.library.arrived + 1
    satellite.return(4242)
}

satellite.library.marks = ""
satellite.library.touched = 0
satellite.library.arrived = 0

satellite.capsule satellite.main(satellite.container.list<satellite.variable.string> arguments)
{
    satellite.variable.number wrong = 0

    // --- §1 -- the deferred call is deferred -----------------------------
    //
    // DESIGN §13: "capsule_name(args) at that position is not performed -- it
    // is PACKAGED. The handler evaluates the arguments and stores (capsule
    // number, argument values) for the thread to run later."
    //
    // THE ARGUMENT IS EVALUATED AND THE CAPSULE IS NOT ENTERED, and those are
    // two separate claims that need two separate witnesses. `"A"` is worked
    // out now; `leave_a_mark` must not have run.
    satellite.variable.thread packaged = satellite.thread.new(leave_a_mark("A"))

    satellite.statement.if (satellite.library.marks != "")
    {
        satellite.console.display("WRONG: new() ran the capsule -- marks is " + satellite.library.marks)
        wrong = wrong + 1
    }

    packaged.start()
    packaged.join()

    satellite.statement.if (satellite.library.marks != "A")
    {
        satellite.console.display("WRONG: after join, marks is " + satellite.library.marks)
        wrong = wrong + 1
    }

    // --- §2 -- start() does not wait, join() does ------------------------
    //
    // `slow_answer` sleeps a quarter second and only then adds one to
    // `arrived`. So the state of that global is a statement about TIME without
    // a clock being involved: if it is still 0 on the line after `start()`,
    // then `start()` did not wait for the sleep; if it is 1 on the line after
    // `join()`, then `join()` did.
    //
    // THE MARGIN IS A QUARTER OF A SECOND AGAINST A FEW MICROSECONDS, which is
    // what makes this a test rather than a race. A machine under enough load
    // to spend 250 ms between two statements has bigger problems than this
    // file, and the failure would be a FALSE ALARM rather than a false pass --
    // which is the direction a flaky assertion is allowed to point.
    satellite.variable.thread timed = satellite.thread.new(slow_answer())
    timed.start()
    satellite.variable.number during = satellite.library.arrived

    satellite.statement.if (during != 0)
    {
        satellite.console.display("WRONG: start() waited for the capsule to finish")
        wrong = wrong + 1
    }

    satellite.variable.number answered = timed.join()
    satellite.variable.number afterwards = satellite.library.arrived

    satellite.statement.if (afterwards != 1)
    {
        satellite.console.display("WRONG: join() came back before the capsule did")
        wrong = wrong + 1
    }

    // --- §5 -- join() answers what the capsule returned ------------------
    //
    // DESIGN gives join() no return value and `example/thread_test.satl`
    // discards it. The author settled it on 2026-09-12: join() is the one of
    // the three verbs that HAS an answer, because it is the one that waits
    // until there is one. MILESTONES/M23.md §2.5.
    satellite.statement.if (answered != 4242)
    {
        satellite.console.display("WRONG: join() answered " + answered.to_string())
        wrong = wrong + 1
    }

    // --- §3 -- DESIGN §7.1's 1600 ----------------------------------------
    //
    // Eight threads, all live at once -- every one started before any is
    // joined, which is what makes them concurrent rather than eight
    // sequential calls wearing threads.
    satellite.container.list<satellite.variable.thread> crew = satellite.container.list()
    satellite.variable.number t = 0
    satellite.statement.for (t = 0; t < 8; t = t + 1)
    {
        crew.append(satellite.thread.new(count_alone(t)))
    }
    // NAMED OUT OF THE LIST BEFORE IT IS ASKED, AND THE NAME COSTS NOTHING
    // BECAUSE A THREAD IS A REFERENCE TYPE. `crew[t].start()` is S0720 -- a
    // selector folds only through a declared name, WORD_NUMBERS §1.5's one hop
    // -- so every element has to be given a name first. For a list that would
    // be a copy; for these it is a second name for the same thread, which is
    // §6's property arriving as a convenience three sections early.
    satellite.statement.for (t = 0; t < 8; t = t + 1)
    {
        satellite.variable.thread member = crew[t]
        member.start()
    }

    satellite.variable.number total = 0
    satellite.statement.for (t = 0; t < 8; t = t + 1)
    {
        satellite.variable.thread member = crew[t]
        total = total + member.join()
    }

    satellite.statement.if (total != 1600)
    {
        satellite.console.display("WRONG: " + (1600 - total).to_string() + " of 1600 came back wrong")
        wrong = wrong + 1
    }

    // --- §4 -- one satellite.library, seen by all of them ----------------
    //
    // Sixteen threads each adding one to a global. The total may be anything
    // from 1 to 16 -- read, add and write are three operations and the
    // language does not fuse them -- but it CANNOT be 0, and a thread whose
    // globals were its own private copy would leave it at exactly 0.
    satellite.container.list<satellite.variable.thread> touchers = satellite.container.list()
    satellite.statement.for (t = 0; t < 16; t = t + 1)
    {
        touchers.append(satellite.thread.new(touch_the_global()))
    }
    satellite.statement.for (t = 0; t < 16; t = t + 1)
    {
        satellite.variable.thread each = touchers[t]
        each.start()
    }
    satellite.statement.for (t = 0; t < 16; t = t + 1)
    {
        satellite.variable.thread each = touchers[t]
        each.join()
    }

    // NAMED BEFORE IT IS ASKED, because a selector folds through a DECLARED
    // name and not through a global's path -- `satellite.library.touched
    // .to_string()` is S0521. That is the language's one-hop rule and not
    // this milestone's, and it is written here because this is the file a
    // reader will copy the idiom out of.
    satellite.variable.number touched = satellite.library.touched

    satellite.statement.if (touched < 1)
    {
        satellite.console.display("WRONG: the threads had their own globals")
        wrong = wrong + 1
    }
    satellite.statement.if (touched > 16)
    {
        satellite.console.display("WRONG: touched is " + touched.to_string())
        wrong = wrong + 1
    }

    // --- §6 -- a thread is a reference type ------------------------------
    //
    // DESIGN §8's table calls a file a reference type and means that two names
    // for one open file ARE one open file. A thread is the same sentence about
    // a different resource: `also` is not a copy of `one`, it is the same
    // thread, so starting through one name and joining through the other
    // reaches the same `pthread_t`.
    //
    // EVERY OTHER VALUE IN THE LANGUAGE GOES THE OTHER WAY, which is what
    // makes this worth asserting rather than assuming -- a list assigned to a
    // second name is a second list, because value.hpp freezes a container's
    // body and publishes every mutation whole.
    satellite.variable.thread one = satellite.thread.new(slow_answer())
    satellite.variable.thread also = one

    satellite.statement.if ((one == also) == satellite.bool.false)
    {
        satellite.console.display("WRONG: two names for one thread compared unequal")
        wrong = wrong + 1
    }

    one.start()
    satellite.variable.number through_the_other = also.join()

    satellite.statement.if (through_the_other != 4242)
    {
        satellite.console.display("WRONG: joining through the second name answered " + through_the_other.to_string())
        wrong = wrong + 1
    }

    satellite.statement.if (wrong == 0)
    {
        satellite.console.display("PASS")
    }
    satellite.statement.else
    {
        satellite.console.display("FAIL")
    }

    satellite.return(satellite)
}
